eia.gov is unreachable from the environment this repository was built in, so
those files were retrieved through third-party GitHub mirrors. The mirrors are
the retrieval path, not the rights holder:

  - Reliability_2013/2014/2015.csv and Sales_Ult_Cust_2013/2014/2015.csv
    via github.com/marcelMerchat/electricPowerReliability1 (no licence stated;
    the mirrored files are the public-domain EIA originals)

  - Reliability_2016/2017/2018.xlsx, Advanced_Meters_2016/2017/2018.xlsx and
    Distribution_Systems_2016/2017/2018.xlsx
    via github.com/IMMM-SFA/electricity_entity_boundaries (BSD 2-Clause,
    Copyright (c) 2019 Integrated Multi-Sector Multi-Scale Modeling)

  - Reliability_2022.xlsx, Advanced_Meters_2022.xlsx,
    Distribution_Systems_2022.xlsx, Frame_2022.xlsx, Sales_Ult_Cust_2022.xlsx
    via github.com/Dex947/distribution-reliability-outage-analytics (MIT)

data/daily/ holds per-snapshot customer counts derived from two public
git-scraping archives of utility outage maps, both built by Simon Willison:

  - pge_outage_map_snapshots.csv, from github.com/simonw/pge-outages-pre-2024
    (Apache License 2.0), which archives Pacific Gas and Electric Company's
    public outage map, 2019-2022

  - fpl_outage_map_snapshots.csv, from github.com/simonw/disaster-data (no
    licence stated), which archives Florida Power & Light Company's public
    county outage page, 2019-2023. Only customer counts, which are facts, are
    redistributed, not the archived documents.

Neither utility produced, reviewed or endorsed this repository's use of them.

